WEBVTT

00:00:00.684 --> 00:00:01.313
Welcome.

00:00:01.949 --> 00:00:04.665
In this lesson, we will turn the six required methods in

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.184
Assignment Three into concrete actions you can follow.

00:00:07.842 --> 00:00:09.303
Imagine a campus festival.

00:00:09.961 --> 00:00:11.875
Some venues need cable connections.

00:00:12.533 --> 00:00:14.395
Some jobs depend on other jobs.

00:00:15.042 --> 00:00:16.535
Some deliveries have a cost.

00:00:17.182 --> 00:00:20.541
These stories produce different graph problems, so we must first decide

00:00:20.556 --> 00:00:21.511
what answer we need.

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:25.316
We will use small examples, follow the changing state, and connect

00:00:25.331 --> 00:00:27.193
the ideas to short Java fragments.

00:00:27.851 --> 00:00:30.165
The complete assignment remains your implementation.

00:00:30.823 --> 00:00:33.560
Keep the contracts beside you, and pause whenever you want to

00:00:33.575 --> 00:00:34.783
predict the next step.

00:00:37.351 --> 00:00:39.983
A vertex represents an object, such as a venue.

00:00:40.630 --> 00:00:42.376
An edge connects two vertices.

00:00:43.023 --> 00:00:45.127
An undirected cable works both ways.

00:00:45.774 --> 00:00:49.080
A directed dependency has an arrow, and that direction matters.

00:00:49.738 --> 00:00:51.937
A weight is a number attached to an edge, such as

00:00:51.952 --> 00:00:52.654
its cost.

00:00:53.301 --> 00:00:55.595
In Assignment Three, vertices start at zero.

00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:59.306
An edge identifier is its position in the input array, also

00:00:59.321 --> 00:01:00.276
starting at zero.

00:01:00.934 --> 00:01:03.628
Sorting the array must preserve that original identifier.

00:01:04.275 --> 00:01:07.201
Two parallel edges may connect the same vertices and still have

00:01:07.216 --> 00:01:08.403
different identifiers.

00:01:09.051 --> 00:01:12.051
We will need those identifiers in both spanning forests and route

00:01:12.066 --> 00:01:12.884
reconstruction.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.911
Here is our route through the lesson.

00:01:17.559 --> 00:01:20.896
Disjoint set union tracks groups as undirected connections arrive.

00:01:21.543 --> 00:01:24.554
Kruskal and Prim build a minimum cost spanning forest using two

00:01:24.569 --> 00:01:25.619
different strategies.

00:01:26.277 --> 00:01:29.298
Kosaraju finds groups in a directed graph where every member can

00:01:29.313 --> 00:01:30.468
reach every other member.

00:01:31.115 --> 00:01:34.916
A depth first topological ordering arranges dependency jobs when no directed

00:01:34.931 --> 00:01:35.844
cycle exists.

00:01:36.492 --> 00:01:40.198
Finally, shortest paths in a directed acyclic graph compute costs from

00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:40.957
one source.

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:44.520
A minimum spanning forest and a shortest path tree answer different

00:01:44.535 --> 00:01:45.248
questions.

00:01:45.905 --> 00:01:48.990
A cheap total cable network does not necessarily give the cheapest

00:01:49.005 --> 00:01:50.487
route from a particular source.

00:01:53.024 --> 00:01:55.349
Start with five vertices and no connections.

00:01:55.997 --> 00:01:58.659
There are five groups, each containing one vertex.

00:01:59.307 --> 00:02:01.948
The parent array represents each group as a rooted tree.

00:02:02.606 --> 00:02:04.004
A root points to itself.

00:02:04.651 --> 00:02:07.757
The find operation follows parent links until it reaches that root,

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:09.686
which acts as the group's representative.

00:02:10.344 --> 00:02:13.576
The representative is an internal choice, not an extra venue or

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:14.999
a required smallest label.

00:02:15.646 --> 00:02:18.077
Initially, every vertex represents itself.

00:02:18.725 --> 00:02:21.155
Set the number of components to five and the largest group

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:21.999
size to one.

00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:25.161
For an empty graph, both quantities are zero.

00:02:25.809 --> 00:02:29.631
Initialization takes linear time because every vertex needs its own state.

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.610
Now process the connection between zero and one.

00:02:35.268 --> 00:02:37.857
Find returns zero for the first vertex and one for the

00:02:37.872 --> 00:02:38.422
second.

00:02:39.063 --> 00:02:41.547
The roots differ, so two groups really will merge.

00:02:42.205 --> 00:02:45.057
With union by size, attach the root of the smaller group

00:02:45.072 --> 00:02:46.354
to the root of the larger group.

00:02:47.001 --> 00:02:49.295
Equal sizes can use either consistent choice.

00:02:49.953 --> 00:02:51.941
Here, one becomes a child of zero.

00:02:52.599 --> 00:02:54.376
Add the sizes at the surviving root.

00:02:55.024 --> 00:02:58.192
The merged size is two, the component count decreases to four,

00:02:58.334 --> 00:02:59.616
and largest becomes two.

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.831
Record the pair four, two after this operation.

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:06.288
The output records a state after every input operation.

00:03:08.856 --> 00:03:10.423
Next, join two and three.

00:03:11.081 --> 00:03:14.407
That creates a second group of size two, leaving three components.

00:03:15.055 --> 00:03:16.326
Largest stays two.

00:03:16.984 --> 00:03:18.234
Then join one and three.

00:03:18.892 --> 00:03:21.892
These arguments are not both roots, so find must first reach

00:03:21.907 --> 00:03:23.821
their representatives, zero and two.

00:03:24.479 --> 00:03:27.901
Joining those roots creates a size four group containing zero, one,

00:03:27.979 --> 00:03:29.029
two, and three.

00:03:29.676 --> 00:03:31.338
Vertex four remains alone.

00:03:31.985 --> 00:03:34.721
The component count becomes two and largest becomes four.

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:37.178
Only root sizes are authoritative.

00:03:37.836 --> 00:03:41.025
Updating size at the original argument instead of the surviving root

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.177
would corrupt later unions, because an argument can already be an

00:03:44.192 --> 00:03:45.527
internal tree vertex.

00:03:48.059 --> 00:03:50.458
Consider a self connection between four and four.

00:03:51.116 --> 00:03:52.798
Both finds return the same root.

00:03:53.445 --> 00:03:56.941
There is nothing to merge, so components and largest stay unchanged.

00:03:57.588 --> 00:03:59.777
The same rule applies to zero and two now.

00:04:00.435 --> 00:04:03.256
Although they are different vertices, their roots already match.

00:04:03.903 --> 00:04:07.103
Decreasing the component count again would count an imaginary merge.

00:04:07.751 --> 00:04:10.772
Record a state for each operation even when it changes nothing.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.324
Our complete sequence therefore ends with two more copies of two,

00:04:14.508 --> 00:04:15.058
four.

00:04:15.604 --> 00:04:19.205
This distinction matters: one output state per operation does not mean

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:21.113
one successful union per operation.

00:04:21.761 --> 00:04:24.835
Always check representatives before changing group statistics.

00:04:27.392 --> 00:04:30.551
Our current parent tree contains the chain three, two, zero.

00:04:31.198 --> 00:04:34.209
Finding three walks along that chain and discovers root zero.

00:04:34.867 --> 00:04:37.983
Path compression then redirects visited vertices toward the root.

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:41.198
Three can point directly to zero on the next search.

00:04:41.845 --> 00:04:45.278
The group has not changed, so neither components nor largest changes.

00:04:45.925 --> 00:04:48.872
If you implement a second iterative pass, save the old next

00:04:48.887 --> 00:04:50.338
parent before overwriting it.

00:04:50.996 --> 00:04:53.110
Otherwise you can lose the remainder of the chain.

00:04:53.757 --> 00:04:57.453
Compression changes the internal representation to make future searches cheaper.

00:04:58.101 --> 00:05:00.268
It does not mean that the graph acquired a new input

00:05:00.283 --> 00:05:01.702
edge between three and zero.

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.902
Path compression and balancing serve different purposes.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.476
Balancing prevents avoidably tall trees when groups merge.

00:05:11.124 --> 00:05:13.544
Compression shortens paths during later searches.

00:05:14.191 --> 00:05:17.708
Assignment Three requires both compression and either union by size or

00:05:17.723 --> 00:05:18.699
union by rank.

00:05:19.346 --> 00:05:23.137
If you use rank, maintain actual component sizes separately because rank

00:05:23.152 --> 00:05:25.551
does not tell you how many vertices a group contains.

00:05:26.198 --> 00:05:28.956
The fragment shows a safe second compression pass after the root

00:05:28.971 --> 00:05:29.810
has been found.

00:05:30.468 --> 00:05:32.224
Notice that next is saved first.

00:05:32.871 --> 00:05:36.409
Across a sequence of operations, the amortized find and union cost

00:05:36.424 --> 00:05:39.150
is inverse Ackermann, which grows extremely slowly.

00:05:39.808 --> 00:05:42.428
It is not a claim that every individual call takes constant

00:05:42.443 --> 00:05:42.993
time.

00:05:45.434 --> 00:05:47.170
Let us close the connectivity part.

00:05:47.817 --> 00:05:50.595
The answer is a sequence of component counts and largest group

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.335
sizes, not the parent array.

00:05:52.993 --> 00:05:56.604
The parent array is internal state, and different valid implementations may

00:05:56.619 --> 00:05:57.627
use different roots.

00:05:58.285 --> 00:06:00.210
Initialization costs order n.

00:06:00.868 --> 00:06:04.216
For q operations with both heuristics, total time is order n

00:06:04.231 --> 00:06:07.178
plus q times inverse Ackermann of n, amortized.

00:06:07.825 --> 00:06:11.068
The arrays use order n memory, and storing the requested output

00:06:11.083 --> 00:06:11.911
adds order q.

00:06:12.042 --> 00:06:16.054
Test an empty graph with no operations, repeated unions, self unions,

00:06:16.132 --> 00:06:17.962
and a new request of a different size.

00:06:18.610 --> 00:06:20.714
Every request must start with fresh state.

00:06:23.267 --> 00:06:26.678
For the next two algorithms, use the same undirected weighted graph.

00:06:27.326 --> 00:06:30.178
The edge from zero to one costs four and has identifier

00:06:30.193 --> 00:06:30.743
zero.

00:06:31.353 --> 00:06:33.710
One to two costs one, identifier one.

00:06:34.357 --> 00:06:36.862
Zero to two costs six, identifier two.

00:06:37.509 --> 00:06:39.961
Two to three costs two, identifier three.

00:06:40.608 --> 00:06:43.124
One to three costs five, identifier four.

00:06:43.782 --> 00:06:45.348
Vertex four is isolated.

00:06:45.995 --> 00:06:47.899
We cannot invent a cable to connect it.

00:06:48.557 --> 00:06:51.884
Therefore the answer is a minimum spanning forest with two components.

00:06:52.531 --> 00:06:55.342
The connected four vertex part needs three chosen edges.

00:06:55.989 --> 00:06:58.968
In general, a graph with n vertices and c components needs

00:06:58.983 --> 00:07:00.518
n minus c forest edges.

00:07:03.101 --> 00:07:05.858
Kruskal sorts all original edges by increasing weight.

00:07:06.506 --> 00:07:10.360
On this example, the identifier order is one, three, zero, four,

00:07:10.427 --> 00:07:10.977
two.

00:07:11.524 --> 00:07:14.777
Notice that sorted position zero belongs to original edge one.

00:07:15.424 --> 00:07:17.128
We keep the original identifier.

00:07:17.786 --> 00:07:19.700
Start a fresh disjoint set structure.

00:07:20.347 --> 00:07:22.915
For each edge, find its endpoint representatives.

00:07:23.563 --> 00:07:27.079
Different representatives mean the edge joins two current trees, so accept

00:07:27.094 --> 00:07:28.344
it and unite those groups.

00:07:28.992 --> 00:07:32.181
Equal representatives mean it would create a cycle, so skip it.

00:07:32.839 --> 00:07:35.249
This comparison must be different, not equal.

00:07:35.896 --> 00:07:38.812
A self loop always has equal representatives and cannot help a

00:07:38.827 --> 00:07:41.279
spanning forest, even if its weight is negative.

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:46.808
Take edge one, connecting one and two with cost one.

00:07:47.456 --> 00:07:50.139
The endpoints belong to different groups, so the edge is safe

00:07:50.154 --> 00:07:50.704
to add.

00:07:51.345 --> 00:07:54.546
Next take edge three, connecting two and three with cost two.

00:07:55.193 --> 00:07:58.604
Vertex two already belongs to the group containing one, but vertex

00:07:58.619 --> 00:07:59.448
three does not.

00:08:00.095 --> 00:08:02.589
This edge joins those groups and brings the running cost to

00:08:02.604 --> 00:08:03.154
three.

00:08:03.732 --> 00:08:05.868
We now have a path from one through two to three.

00:08:06.515 --> 00:08:09.252
A useful invariant is that the accepted edges always form a

00:08:09.267 --> 00:08:09.874
forest.

00:08:10.521 --> 00:08:14.417
The disjoint set structure represents exactly its connected components, which makes

00:08:14.432 --> 00:08:15.682
the cycle test efficient.

00:08:18.220 --> 00:08:20.683
Edge zero costs four and connects zero to one.

00:08:21.341 --> 00:08:24.393
Vertex zero is still outside the current tree, so accept the

00:08:24.408 --> 00:08:26.312
edge and increase total cost to seven.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:29.654
The four connected venues are now linked with three edges.

00:08:30.312 --> 00:08:34.324
The remaining candidates, identifiers four and two, connect vertices already in

00:08:34.339 --> 00:08:35.188
this same group.

00:08:35.836 --> 00:08:37.971
Both would create cycles, so skip them.

00:08:38.619 --> 00:08:40.733
Vertex four remains its own component.

00:08:41.381 --> 00:08:45.350
One accepted result is cost seven, edge identifiers one, three, zero,

00:08:45.439 --> 00:08:46.521
and components two.

00:08:47.168 --> 00:08:49.725
The order of returned identifiers is unrestricted.

00:08:50.383 --> 00:08:53.299
A validator must check the forest's meaning rather than require this

00:08:53.314 --> 00:08:54.617
particular list ordering.

00:08:57.162 --> 00:09:00.225
Why does choosing a cheap eligible edge preserve an optimal answer?

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:04.168
Imagine a cut separating a current component from the other vertices.

00:09:04.815 --> 00:09:06.846
A lightest edge crossing that cut is safe.

00:09:07.493 --> 00:09:10.209
If an optimum forest lacks it, add it to that optimum.

00:09:10.856 --> 00:09:14.467
Within the relevant connected component, this creates a cycle containing another

00:09:14.482 --> 00:09:15.353
crossing edge.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:18.294
Replacing that edge cannot increase the cost.

00:09:18.941 --> 00:09:21.436
This exchange argument supports the greedy choice.

00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:24.524
Ties can therefore yield different correct forests.

00:09:25.172 --> 00:09:26.717
Negative weights are valid too.

00:09:27.364 --> 00:09:31.071
Sorting dominates Kruskal's time, and the extra n term handles isolated

00:09:31.086 --> 00:09:31.777
vertices.

00:09:32.424 --> 00:09:35.393
Use a signed sixty four bit total, because many legal weights

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.027
can sum beyond thirty two bits.

00:09:39.586 --> 00:09:42.301
Prim solves the same forest problem through a different state.

00:09:42.949 --> 00:09:45.738
Instead of sorting all edges globally, it grows a tree from

00:09:45.753 --> 00:09:46.824
a visited vertex.

00:09:47.471 --> 00:09:48.468
Start at zero.

00:09:49.116 --> 00:09:52.601
Offer its outgoing undirected adjacency entries to a priority queue.

00:09:53.259 --> 00:09:55.837
The candidates are edge zero with weight four and edge two

00:09:55.852 --> 00:09:56.765
with weight six.

00:09:57.412 --> 00:09:59.379
Remove the cheapest eligible candidate.

00:10:00.037 --> 00:10:03.353
Edge zero reaches unvisited vertex one, so add it and mark

00:10:03.368 --> 00:10:04.134
one visited.

00:10:04.781 --> 00:10:07.043
The growing tree now contains zero and one.

00:10:07.691 --> 00:10:11.070
The heap should preserve the original edge identifier alongside the candidate

00:10:11.085 --> 00:10:12.293
destination and weight.

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:16.857
This is a genuinely separate implementation from Kruskal, using adjacency lists

00:10:16.872 --> 00:10:17.606
and a heap.

00:10:20.169 --> 00:10:23.538
When vertex one enters the tree, offer its edges toward unvisited

00:10:23.553 --> 00:10:24.245
vertices.

00:10:24.892 --> 00:10:27.628
New candidates include one to two with cost one and one

00:10:27.643 --> 00:10:28.957
to three with cost five.

00:10:29.604 --> 00:10:32.309
The earlier zero to two candidate with cost six stays in

00:10:32.324 --> 00:10:32.874
the queue.

00:10:33.494 --> 00:10:35.556
Choose the cost one edge and visit two.

00:10:36.203 --> 00:10:39.182
Then two exposes its cost two edge to three, which becomes

00:10:39.197 --> 00:10:40.880
the next cheapest eligible choice.

00:10:41.527 --> 00:10:44.991
The accepted costs are four, one, and two, totaling seven.

00:10:45.649 --> 00:10:48.680
The sequence differs from Kruskal's sequence, but it reaches the same

00:10:48.695 --> 00:10:49.619
optimum cost.

00:10:50.266 --> 00:10:52.739
The cheapest edge changes as the boundary of the growing tree

00:10:52.754 --> 00:10:53.414
changes.

00:10:55.956 --> 00:10:59.030
A lazy priority queue can still contain old entries for vertices

00:10:59.045 --> 00:11:00.432
that have already entered the tree.

00:11:01.090 --> 00:11:04.322
For example, the cost six edge toward two becomes stale after

00:11:04.337 --> 00:11:05.692
the cheaper route adds two.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.317
When that entry is removed, skip it.

00:11:08.975 --> 00:11:10.162
Never charge it again.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:14.010
Once the heap empties, the current connected component is complete.

00:11:14.657 --> 00:11:17.910
Scan for another unvisited vertex and start a new component.

00:11:18.558 --> 00:11:19.966
Here that vertex is four.

00:11:20.613 --> 00:11:22.633
It contributes a component and no edge.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.018
If you start only once, your implementation can accidentally ignore disconnected

00:11:27.033 --> 00:11:27.619
pieces.

00:11:28.277 --> 00:11:32.553
The final forest must cover every vertex, including all isolated vertices.

00:11:35.122 --> 00:11:38.439
Kruskal and Prim should agree on minimum cost and feasibility, although

00:11:38.454 --> 00:11:40.621
ties can produce different edge identifiers.

00:11:41.268 --> 00:11:43.731
They must remain different algorithms in your submission.

00:11:44.389 --> 00:11:47.125
For a lazy heap, Prim takes order n plus m log

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:49.908
of m plus one time, and order n plus m memory.

00:11:50.556 --> 00:11:52.965
An indexed heap can give the alternative bound shown in the

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:53.766
contracts.

00:11:54.414 --> 00:11:58.584
Verify the result structurally: no repeated chosen identifier, no loop, no

00:11:58.599 --> 00:12:01.968
cycle, the correct number of components, and exactly n minus c

00:12:01.983 --> 00:12:02.938
selected edges.

00:12:03.585 --> 00:12:06.859
Matching the cost alone is insufficient, because an invalid set of

00:12:06.874 --> 00:12:09.052
edges can coincidentally have the same sum.

00:12:11.622 --> 00:12:13.326
Now switch to a directed graph.

00:12:13.973 --> 00:12:16.615
Zero points to one, and one points back to zero.

00:12:17.273 --> 00:12:19.440
Both zero and one also lead toward two.

00:12:20.098 --> 00:12:22.265
Two and three point to each other, and three points to

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:22.830
four.

00:12:23.376 --> 00:12:24.932
Vertex five is isolated.

00:12:25.580 --> 00:12:28.822
A strongly connected component is a maximal group where every vertex

00:12:28.837 --> 00:12:31.373
can reach every other vertex along directed paths.

00:12:32.021 --> 00:12:33.756
Zero and one form one group.

00:12:34.403 --> 00:12:35.695
Two and three form another.

00:12:36.343 --> 00:12:38.510
Four and five each form a singleton group.

00:12:39.157 --> 00:12:41.303
Having a path from the first group into the second does

00:12:41.318 --> 00:12:43.612
not merge them, because there is no path back.

00:12:46.206 --> 00:12:49.649
Kosaraju uses two depth first passes and a transposed graph.

00:12:50.297 --> 00:12:53.413
In this orientation, run the first pass on the original graph.

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:57.049
We choose vertices and adjacency entries in the displayed order.

00:12:57.697 --> 00:13:01.182
Starting at zero, the search follows one, then two, then three,

00:13:01.281 --> 00:13:01.951
then four.

00:13:02.599 --> 00:13:05.293
Four has no outgoing work, so it finishes first.

00:13:05.941 --> 00:13:09.478
Returning through the search finishes three, two, one, and zero.

00:13:10.126 --> 00:13:13.210
Append each vertex at finish time, not at discovery time.

00:13:13.857 --> 00:13:15.614
Finally visit isolated five.

00:13:16.261 --> 00:13:17.901
Its search immediately finishes.

00:13:18.549 --> 00:13:21.844
A different adjacency order may change this list, but the algorithm's

00:13:21.859 --> 00:13:23.963
rule is always to record completed work.

00:13:26.539 --> 00:13:30.825
The recorded finish list is four, three, two, one, zero, five.

00:13:31.473 --> 00:13:35.580
Reverse it to obtain decreasing finish order: five, zero, one, two,

00:13:35.689 --> 00:13:36.665
three, four.

00:13:37.313 --> 00:13:40.397
Build the transpose by reversing every original directed edge.

00:13:41.045 --> 00:13:43.286
Clear the visited state before the second pass.

00:13:43.944 --> 00:13:46.227
Then take seeds from this reversed finish list.

00:13:46.874 --> 00:13:50.486
A new second pass search collects one complete strongly connected component.

00:13:51.133 --> 00:13:54.734
Reusing the original edge direction for both passes can incorrectly cross

00:13:54.749 --> 00:13:55.767
between components.

00:13:56.414 --> 00:13:59.889
Using discovery order instead of finish order also breaks the reasoning.

00:14:00.547 --> 00:14:03.515
You may swap which graph is used first, provided the second

00:14:03.530 --> 00:14:06.425
pass uses its transpose with the corresponding finish order.

00:14:08.963 --> 00:14:10.635
Follow the second pass carefully.

00:14:11.293 --> 00:14:13.639
Seed five in the transpose finds only five.

00:14:14.287 --> 00:14:17.582
Seed zero reaches one and stays within their component, so assign

00:14:17.597 --> 00:14:18.594
both the same label.

00:14:19.241 --> 00:14:21.988
Seed one is already visited and starts no new search.

00:14:22.636 --> 00:14:24.191
Seed two collects two and three.

00:14:24.849 --> 00:14:26.479
Seed three is already visited.

00:14:27.126 --> 00:14:29.009
Seed four forms its own singleton.

00:14:29.667 --> 00:14:32.077
The order is designed to prevent a new search from spilling

00:14:32.092 --> 00:14:33.837
into a component that should be separate.

00:14:34.485 --> 00:14:37.316
One implementation may assign labels in discovery order.

00:14:37.974 --> 00:14:40.173
Another may use different nonnegative integers.

00:14:40.831 --> 00:14:41.954
The labels are names.

00:14:42.602 --> 00:14:45.728
Their equality pattern, meaning which vertices share a label, is what

00:14:45.743 --> 00:14:46.298
matters.

00:14:48.832 --> 00:14:53.033
Use labels seven, seven, four, four, nine, two for vertices zero

00:14:53.048 --> 00:14:53.771
through five.

00:14:54.419 --> 00:14:56.322
Inspect every original edge again.

00:14:56.970 --> 00:15:00.075
An edge whose endpoints share a label stays inside one component

00:15:00.090 --> 00:15:01.910
and must not appear in the condensation.

00:15:02.557 --> 00:15:04.735
Edges from zero to two and from one to two both

00:15:04.750 --> 00:15:06.717
become the same pair, seven to four.

00:15:07.375 --> 00:15:09.004
Include that pair only once.

00:15:09.652 --> 00:15:11.924
The edge from three to four becomes four to nine.

00:15:12.572 --> 00:15:16.004
Isolated label two remains represented by the component array even though

00:15:16.019 --> 00:15:17.965
it contributes no condensation edge.

00:15:18.612 --> 00:15:20.347
The condensation is acyclic.

00:15:20.995 --> 00:15:24.617
It summarizes component dependencies without pretending that the original internal

00:15:24.632 --> 00:15:25.839
cycles have disappeared.

00:15:28.415 --> 00:15:32.121
The key ingredients are two passes, a transpose, and decreasing finish

00:15:32.136 --> 00:15:33.470
order for the second pass.

00:15:34.128 --> 00:15:38.414
Building adjacency lists, traversing each graph, and examining original edges all

00:15:38.429 --> 00:15:39.416
take linear work.

00:15:40.063 --> 00:15:43.390
With a hash set for condensation pairs, the expected total time

00:15:43.405 --> 00:15:44.550
is order n plus m.

00:15:44.681 --> 00:15:47.986
Memory is also order n plus m, including the returned arrays

00:15:48.001 --> 00:15:48.977
and condensation.

00:15:49.635 --> 00:15:52.994
Deep chains can exceed a language's recursion stack, so an explicit

00:15:53.009 --> 00:15:55.313
stack is a robust choice at the published limits.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:59.066
A DFS frame needs enough information to resume the next neighbor

00:15:59.081 --> 00:16:01.533
and recognize when the vertex has actually finished.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:05.728
A plain stack of vertices alone does not automatically reproduce recursive

00:16:05.743 --> 00:16:06.477
finish order.

00:16:09.017 --> 00:16:12.417
A topological order lists every vertex exactly once so that every

00:16:12.432 --> 00:16:15.264
directed edge goes from an earlier position to a later one.

00:16:15.911 --> 00:16:19.280
In our scheduling example, jobs zero and one both precede job

00:16:19.295 --> 00:16:21.547
two, and job two precedes job three.

00:16:22.194 --> 00:16:25.247
Zero and one can exchange positions because neither depends on the

00:16:25.262 --> 00:16:25.812
other.

00:16:26.337 --> 00:16:28.420
That means more than one answer can be valid.

00:16:29.067 --> 00:16:31.825
The task requires a depth first method, so we will use

00:16:31.840 --> 00:16:33.807
reverse postorder with cycle detection.

00:16:34.454 --> 00:16:37.497
A vertex without incident edges still belongs in the output.

00:16:38.154 --> 00:16:41.228
The empty graph also has a valid topological order, which is

00:16:41.243 --> 00:16:42.388
simply an empty list.

00:16:44.934 --> 00:16:46.785
Give each vertex one of three states.

00:16:47.432 --> 00:16:49.420
White means the search has never entered it.

00:16:50.078 --> 00:16:52.699
Gray means it is active on the current depth first path.

00:16:53.357 --> 00:16:55.566
Black means all its outgoing work has finished.

00:16:56.214 --> 00:16:57.790
These distinctions are essential.

00:16:58.448 --> 00:17:00.742
An edge to a white vertex starts more work.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.831
An edge to a gray vertex points back into the active

00:17:03.846 --> 00:17:05.613
path and proves a directed cycle.

00:17:06.270 --> 00:17:09.144
An edge to a black vertex merely reaches completed work and

00:17:09.159 --> 00:17:10.841
does not by itself prove a cycle.

00:17:11.489 --> 00:17:14.615
A single visited Boolean loses the difference between gray and black.

00:17:15.273 --> 00:17:17.957
Our example will show exactly why that difference matters.

00:17:20.517 --> 00:17:22.336
Start depth first search at zero.

00:17:22.994 --> 00:17:24.413
Visit two and then three.

00:17:25.060 --> 00:17:27.649
Three finishes first, then two, then zero.

00:17:28.307 --> 00:17:30.664
The finish list is now three, two, zero.

00:17:31.322 --> 00:17:34.164
Vertex one is still white, so start another search there.

00:17:34.822 --> 00:17:37.474
Its edge reaches two, which is already black, so there is

00:17:37.489 --> 00:17:38.813
no recursive work left.

00:17:39.461 --> 00:17:40.858
Finish one and append it.

00:17:41.516 --> 00:17:45.191
Reversing the complete finish list gives one, zero, two, three.

00:17:45.838 --> 00:17:49.386
Check each dependency: both initial jobs appear before two, and two

00:17:49.401 --> 00:17:50.504
appears before three.

00:17:51.151 --> 00:17:54.436
Recording vertices when they are first entered would produce preorder, which

00:17:54.451 --> 00:17:56.903
is not the required reverse postorder construction.

00:17:59.441 --> 00:18:01.313
Let us isolate the common false alarm.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:04.750
When the search finally starts from one, its neighbor two has

00:18:04.765 --> 00:18:06.500
already finished in the earlier search.

00:18:07.147 --> 00:18:09.789
The graph still has a perfectly valid dependency order.

00:18:10.436 --> 00:18:13.605
Reporting a cycle whenever a neighbor is merely visited would incorrectly

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:14.311
reject it.

00:18:14.958 --> 00:18:17.157
Only a gray neighbor lies on the active path that can

00:18:17.172 --> 00:18:18.349
close a directed loop.

00:18:18.996 --> 00:18:21.912
In iterative code, mark a vertex black only when all its

00:18:21.927 --> 00:18:23.156
neighbors have been examined.

00:18:23.803 --> 00:18:25.770
If you mark it finished as soon as you push it,

00:18:25.975 --> 00:18:28.869
cycle detection and finish order can both become incorrect.

00:18:29.517 --> 00:18:32.095
The state must reflect the actual stage of the search.

00:18:34.636 --> 00:18:36.993
Now add a dependency from three back to zero.

00:18:37.651 --> 00:18:41.189
During the search path zero, two, three, vertex zero is still

00:18:41.204 --> 00:18:41.754
gray.

00:18:42.311 --> 00:18:45.184
The edge from three to zero returns to that active ancestor

00:18:45.199 --> 00:18:46.681
and closes a directed cycle.

00:18:47.329 --> 00:18:50.698
No ordering can place zero before two, two before three, and

00:18:50.713 --> 00:18:52.574
three before zero simultaneously.

00:18:53.222 --> 00:18:55.494
Return acyclic false and an empty order.

00:18:56.152 --> 00:18:59.553
Search every component, because a cycle in a disconnected component still

00:18:59.568 --> 00:19:00.723
invalidates the graph.

00:19:01.370 --> 00:19:02.990
A self loop is also a cycle.

00:19:03.637 --> 00:19:05.772
Do not return a partial finish list as though it were

00:19:05.787 --> 00:19:08.798
a usable schedule after detecting a cycle anywhere in the graph.

00:19:11.334 --> 00:19:14.682
For a deep graph, an explicit frame can reproduce recursive depth

00:19:14.697 --> 00:19:15.599
first behavior.

00:19:16.247 --> 00:19:18.783
Each frame stores a vertex and the index of the next

00:19:18.798 --> 00:19:19.763
neighbor to examine.

00:19:20.411 --> 00:19:23.305
If a neighbor remains, advance that index before descending.

00:19:23.963 --> 00:19:26.900
If no neighbor remains, mark the vertex black, append it to

00:19:26.915 --> 00:19:28.671
the finish list, and pop the frame.

00:19:29.318 --> 00:19:32.624
After every component has been checked successfully, reverse the list.

00:19:33.272 --> 00:19:36.662
Every vertex enters and exits once, and every edge is examined

00:19:36.677 --> 00:19:38.960
once, giving order n plus m time.

00:19:39.618 --> 00:19:43.356
The adjacency lists, color array, frame stack, and output fit in

00:19:43.371 --> 00:19:44.747
order n plus m memory.

00:19:45.395 --> 00:19:48.237
This also avoids relying on a small native call stack.

00:19:50.781 --> 00:19:53.770
The final algorithm asks a new question: what is the cheapest

00:19:53.785 --> 00:19:56.301
directed route from a chosen source to each vertex?

00:19:56.948 --> 00:19:57.977
Use source zero.

00:19:58.635 --> 00:20:01.287
The input edges are zero to one with cost three, zero

00:20:01.302 --> 00:20:03.880
to two with cost eight, one to two with cost minus

00:20:03.895 --> 00:20:05.925
seven, and two to three with cost four.

00:20:06.583 --> 00:20:10.637
Their original identifiers are zero, one, two, and three respectively.

00:20:11.285 --> 00:20:13.336
Vertex four has no route from zero.

00:20:13.983 --> 00:20:16.825
This graph is acyclic, even though one edge has a negative

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:17.390
weight.

00:20:17.958 --> 00:20:19.355
The negative edge is valid.

00:20:20.003 --> 00:20:24.278
Acyclicity, rather than nonnegative weights, is what makes topological relaxation

00:20:24.293 --> 00:20:25.090
sufficient here.

00:20:27.639 --> 00:20:31.524
Before computing the distances, check the entire directed graph for cycles.

00:20:32.172 --> 00:20:35.066
A search only from the source is insufficient for the contract.

00:20:35.714 --> 00:20:38.914
Imagine an additional disconnected pair of vertices with arrows in both

00:20:38.929 --> 00:20:39.673
directions.

00:20:40.321 --> 00:20:43.152
The source cannot reach that pair, but the input graph still

00:20:43.167 --> 00:20:44.965
is not a directed acyclic graph.

00:20:45.613 --> 00:20:49.266
Return acyclic false, an empty distance array, and an empty parent

00:20:49.281 --> 00:20:49.909
edge array.

00:20:50.567 --> 00:20:52.197
The same applies to a self loop.

00:20:52.844 --> 00:20:56.160
This requirement is stronger than asking whether the source's reachable region

00:20:56.175 --> 00:20:57.510
happens to be acyclic.

00:20:58.157 --> 00:21:01.495
Reuse a correctly implemented whole graph depth first ordering rather than

00:21:01.510 --> 00:21:03.614
quietly ignoring unreachable components.

00:21:06.158 --> 00:21:10.065
After acyclicity succeeds, initialize the source distance to zero and all

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:11.277
other distances to null.

00:21:11.924 --> 00:21:14.271
Initialize every parent edge to minus one.

00:21:14.918 --> 00:21:17.012
A null distance means we do not yet have a source

00:21:17.027 --> 00:21:20.037
route, so no relaxation may start from that vertex.

00:21:20.695 --> 00:21:23.990
The integer minus one cannot serve as the unreachable distance because

00:21:24.005 --> 00:21:27.322
negative edges can produce a legitimate shortest distance of minus one.

00:21:27.969 --> 00:21:31.180
Notice that the distance sentinel and parent sentinel have different roles.

00:21:31.827 --> 00:21:33.752
Distances use null for unreachable.

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.252
Parent edges use minus one for the source and for unreachable

00:21:37.267 --> 00:21:37.979
vertices.

00:21:38.627 --> 00:21:42.059
Use sufficiently wide integer arithmetic for valid finite sums.

00:21:44.610 --> 00:21:48.422
Take a valid topological order such as zero, one, two, three,

00:21:48.500 --> 00:21:49.050
four.

00:21:49.617 --> 00:21:50.878
Process zero first.

00:21:51.525 --> 00:21:55.094
Edge zero offers cost three to vertex one, so record distance

00:21:55.109 --> 00:21:56.476
three and parent edge zero.

00:21:57.123 --> 00:22:00.186
Edge one offers cost eight to vertex two, so record eight

00:22:00.201 --> 00:22:01.240
and parent edge one.

00:22:01.898 --> 00:22:03.423
Now process vertex one.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:06.016
Its distance is already final at three.

00:22:06.663 --> 00:22:09.537
Following the negative edge to two gives three plus minus seven,

00:22:09.731 --> 00:22:10.960
which equals minus four.

00:22:11.618 --> 00:22:13.437
That improves the earlier cost eight.

00:22:14.085 --> 00:22:16.252
Replace both the distance and the parent edge.

00:22:16.899 --> 00:22:20.574
The new parent is original edge identifier two, not predecessor vertex

00:22:20.589 --> 00:22:22.229
one and not a sorted position.

00:22:24.776 --> 00:22:26.005
Next process two.

00:22:26.653 --> 00:22:29.811
Its outgoing edge reaches three with candidate cost minus four plus

00:22:29.826 --> 00:22:31.297
four, which equals zero.

00:22:31.945 --> 00:22:34.249
Record distance zero and parent edge three.

00:22:34.907 --> 00:22:36.758
Vertex four remains unreachable.

00:22:37.405 --> 00:22:38.655
Why is one pass enough?

00:22:39.303 --> 00:22:42.914
Every predecessor of a vertex appears earlier in a topological order.

00:22:43.562 --> 00:22:46.815
By the time that vertex is processed, all possible incoming route

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:48.607
improvements have already been considered.

00:22:49.254 --> 00:22:52.296
Its final distance can then safely extend to its successors.

00:22:52.954 --> 00:22:56.692
This reasoning also handles negative weights, because no later vertex can

00:22:56.707 --> 00:22:58.073
lead back to an earlier one.

00:22:58.721 --> 00:23:02.754
With adjacency lists, cycle checking and all relaxations together take order

00:23:02.769 --> 00:23:04.219
n plus m time and memory.

00:23:06.776 --> 00:23:10.040
To reconstruct a route to three, read its parent edge identifier,

00:23:10.118 --> 00:23:10.841
which is three.

00:23:11.488 --> 00:23:13.866
In the input, edge three runs from two to three.

00:23:14.524 --> 00:23:15.427
Move to two.

00:23:16.074 --> 00:23:18.557
Its parent edge is two, running from one to two.

00:23:19.205 --> 00:23:20.065
Move to one.

00:23:20.723 --> 00:23:23.428
Its parent edge is zero, running from zero to one.

00:23:24.075 --> 00:23:25.325
We have reached the source.

00:23:25.973 --> 00:23:29.848
The collected vertices run backward as three, two, one, zero, so

00:23:29.863 --> 00:23:32.705
reverse them to obtain zero, one, two, three.

00:23:33.352 --> 00:23:36.352
Check the direction and sum the chosen input edges: three plus

00:23:36.367 --> 00:23:38.281
minus seven plus four equals zero.

00:23:38.939 --> 00:23:41.918
The helper takes time and output space proportional to the returned

00:23:41.933 --> 00:23:42.646
route length.

00:23:45.194 --> 00:23:47.003
A shortest route need not be unique.

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:50.387
In a modified example, add another zero to one edge of

00:23:50.402 --> 00:23:51.156
cost three.

00:23:51.814 --> 00:23:55.226
Either of those original identifiers can correctly parent vertex one.

00:23:55.873 --> 00:23:58.609
Add a direct zero to three edge of cost zero, and

00:23:58.624 --> 00:24:00.560
the direct route ties with our longer route.

00:24:01.207 --> 00:24:03.733
A semantic checker should accept either valid optimum.

00:24:04.391 --> 00:24:08.487
Preserve the actual edge identifier so parallel choices remain unambiguous.

00:24:09.135 --> 00:24:11.945
Your helper must also return the single vertex source route when

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:13.168
target equals source.

00:24:13.815 --> 00:24:16.942
For unreachable four, use and document a no route result.

00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:20.357
Actually call all three helper cases in your own tests and

00:24:20.372 --> 00:24:22.476
record their results in the English analysis.

00:24:25.029 --> 00:24:27.175
Before timing anything, test correctness.

00:24:27.823 --> 00:24:32.109
Predict small answers independently, rather than copying the implementation's output

00:24:32.124 --> 00:24:33.374
into an expected file.

00:24:34.021 --> 00:24:38.033
For a forest, check its original identifiers, connectivity, edge count, and

00:24:38.048 --> 00:24:38.856
total cost.

00:24:39.503 --> 00:24:43.473
For strongly connected components, check the partition and the exact deduplicated

00:24:43.488 --> 00:24:44.348
condensation.

00:24:44.995 --> 00:24:49.376
For a topological order, build vertex positions and verify every dependency.

00:24:50.034 --> 00:24:53.635
For shortest paths, validate parent directions and route costs as well

00:24:53.650 --> 00:24:54.521
as distances.

00:24:55.168 --> 00:24:58.685
Include tied optima, loops where allowed, empty graphs where the contract

00:24:58.700 --> 00:25:00.255
allows them, and deep chains.

00:25:00.913 --> 00:25:04.187
Then send several different requests through one running process to expose

00:25:04.202 --> 00:25:06.475
state that accidentally leaks between tasks.

00:25:09.029 --> 00:25:12.324
For the required Kruskal versus Prim benchmark, use the same graph

00:25:12.339 --> 00:25:14.528
instances and environment for both methods.

00:25:15.175 --> 00:25:18.586
Verify correct costs and valid forests before comparing speed.

00:25:19.234 --> 00:25:22.465
Choose three sizes and report their vertex and edge counts, plus

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:25.069
a reproducible generator seed or saved inputs.

00:25:25.727 --> 00:25:28.696
At each size, measure each method at least three times.

00:25:29.354 --> 00:25:33.271
The illustrative Kruskal values twelve, ten, eleven have median eleven.

00:25:33.918 --> 00:25:37.066
The Prim values nine, thirteen, ten have median ten.

00:25:37.713 --> 00:25:40.439
These are teaching numbers, not measurements of your program.

00:25:41.097 --> 00:25:44.793
Exclude compilation, document warm up, and use the same timing boundary

00:25:44.808 --> 00:25:45.752
for both methods.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:49.421
A few observed timings do not prove an asymptotic bound.

00:25:51.976 --> 00:25:55.545
