00:27:17.309 --> 00:27:19.951
You now have a worked example for every required method.

00:27:20.598 --> 00:27:23.567
When writing your solution, ask what the state means before writing

00:27:23.582 --> 00:27:24.358
the next loop.

00:27:25.005 --> 00:27:27.436
In disjoint set union, it represents groups.

00:27:28.094 --> 00:27:31.431
In Kruskal, it certifies that accepted edges stay acyclic.

00:27:32.078 --> 00:27:35.405
In Prim, the heap describes candidate edges leaving a growing tree.

00:27:36.063 --> 00:27:39.137
In Kosaraju, finish order coordinates the two passes.

00:27:39.795 --> 00:27:43.670
In topological sorting, gray distinguishes active work from completed work.

00:27:44.318 --> 00:27:48.224
In directed acyclic shortest paths, the ordering makes every processed distance

00:27:48.239 --> 00:27:48.789
final.

00:27:49.325 --> 00:27:52.515
